    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d516">

                <pb facs="0702" n="602" sameAs="frus:frus1934v02/pg602"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">211.65/101: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Ambassador in Italy</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic">Long</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1934-04-05T13:00:00-05:00">April 5, 1934—1 p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>24. Your 51, April 3, 11 a.m. You may inform the Italian Foreign Office that this
                    Government is willing to defer to its desire that the negotiations be conducted
                    in Rome.</p>
                <p>The Embassy’s despatch No. 2165 of February 28, 1929,<anchor n="17"
                        xml:id="d516-f1"/> states that on January 18, 1929, the Embassy delivered to
                    the Minister of Foreign Affairs a draft extradition convention, and that the
                    competent Italian authorities were studying that draft. The Department’s files
                    do not indicate, however, that the Italian Government has made any comment on
                    that draft. We should prefer that the matter be taken up where it was dropped in
                    1929 rather than to begin now by studying the Italian draft mentioned in your
                    telegram. Please ascertain whether this procedure would be agreeable to the
                    Italian Government.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
